    "display_title": "Mindfulness Training Modifies Subsystems of Attention",
    "authors": [
      "Amishi P. Jha",
      "Jason Krompinger",
      "Michael J. Baime"
    ]
  },
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Mindfulness training can improve attention-related behavioral responses by changing specific subcomponents of attention rather than producing one uniform attentional benefit.",
      "short_title": "Training modifies attention subsystems",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The study examined three functionally and neuroanatomically distinct but overlapping attentional subsystems--alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring--using the Attention Network Test before and after mindfulness training."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Participants with prior concentrative meditation experience showed reduced conflict-monitoring interference at baseline compared with meditation-naive participants.",
      "short_title": "Experience reduces conflict interference",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "At Time 1, the collapsed MBSR and control groups differed from the retreat group on conflict monitoring for response time, t(1,49)=2.22, p=.03, and accuracy, t(1,49)=5.62, p<.001; the conflict-monitoring effect was reduced in the retreat group."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "An 8-week MBSR course improved endogenous orienting performance at Time 2 relative to both controls and retreat participants.",
      "short_title": "MBSR improves endogenous orienting",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "At Time 2, orienting response-time difference scores showed a significant group effect, F(2,48)=10.13, p<.001; the MBSR group differed from controls after Bonferroni adjustment, p=.046, and from the retreat group, p<.001."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "After retreat training, experienced meditators detected targets more efficiently when no warning cue was provided, consistent with enhanced exogenous alerting readiness.",
      "short_title": "Retreat improves no-cue detection",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "At Time 2, alerting response-time scores differed between the retreat group and the collapsed control plus MBSR group, t(1,49)=3.55, p<.001; younger retreat participants had significantly shorter no-cue response times than the comparison group, t(1,40)=2.17, p=.03."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Conflict-monitoring differences were not observed across groups at Time 2, plausibly because task exposure improved performance across all groups and masked training-specific gains.",
      "short_title": "Conflict gains equalize over time",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "At Time 2, the conflict-monitoring response-time and accuracy difference scores had no significant main effects and no significant paired contrasts, p>.36; the authors report that control participants improved in conflict monitoring from Time 1 to Time 2 without attention training."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "More months of prior meditation experience in the retreat group predicted smaller alerting scores at Time 2.",
      "short_title": "Experience reduces alerting scores",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "For retreat participants, months of meditation experience correlated with Time 2 alerting score, R=-.52, t=2.4, p=.03, indicating a significant reduction in alerting score with increased meditation experience."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The study interprets concentrative practice as primarily affecting dorsal attention functions and receptive practice as primarily affecting ventral alerting functions.",
      "short_title": "Practice maps attention systems",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The discussion links conflict monitoring and orienting to voluntary attentional selection in the dorsal attention system, while receptive attention is described as corresponding to ventral attention functions of exogenous stimulus detection, attentional readiness, and alerting."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The reported findings are preliminary because the training groups were small, the controls did not receive an active alternative training program, and stress-reduction mechanisms were not separated from attention-training mechanisms.",
      "short_title": "Small samples limit inference",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The discussion states that the results should be interpreted cautiously because the experiment was among the first to evaluate mindfulness training and attentional subsystems, training groups were relatively small, and future work should include active-training controls, neural measures, and better characterization of training protocols."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_items": [
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-ant-three-subsystems-design--e5f6a7b8c9d0",
      "title": "ANT Subsystem Design",
      "excerpt": "Participants in MBSR, retreat, and control groups performed the Attention Network Test at Time 1 and Time 2. The task used four cue conditions--no cue, double cue, center cue, and spatial cue--and two target conditions--congruent and incongruent flankers--with response time and accuracy difference scores used to index alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring.",
      "overview": "This design is the operational backbone of the paper. It connects mindfulness-training hypotheses to separable behavioral markers rather than relying on self-report, allowing the authors to ask whether training changes alerting, orienting, and conflict monitoring differently. The design supports broad claims about subsystem-specific effects, while its behavioral-only nature limits direct neural conclusions.",
      "evidence_type": "model_constraint",
