      ],
      "source_id": "source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78",
      "page_refs": [
        "pp.114-115",
        "Figure 3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-retreat-alerting-time-two--c3d4e5f6a7b8",
      "title": "Retreat Alerting Shift",
      "excerpt": "At Time 2, alerting response-time scores significantly differed between the retreat group and the collapsed control plus MBSR group, t(1,49)=3.55, p<.001, while accuracy did not differ, p>.7. In the younger retreat comparison, no-cue response times were shorter than controls plus MBSR, t(1,40)=2.17, p=.03, but double-cue response times were not, p>.6.",
      "overview": "This result grounds the paper's receptive-attention interpretation. The reduced alerting score is not treated as reduced alertness; it reflects better target detection when no temporal or spatial warning cue is available. Because the explicit no-cue comparison uses the younger retreat subgroup, age remains an important scope condition.",
      "evidence_type": "statistical_result",
