  "source_dossier": {
    "summary_short": "This article tests whether mindfulness training changes distinct attentional subsystems rather than attention in general. MBSR, intensive retreat practice, and no-training control groups completed the Attention Network Test before and after the training interval. The main pattern links novice MBSR training with improved orienting and experienced retreat practice with altered alerting readiness, while conflict-monitoring effects were strongest at baseline in experienced meditators.",
    "summary_long": "Jha, Krompinger, and Baime investigate mindfulness training as a behavioral attention-training protocol by separating attention into alerting, orienting, and conflict-monitoring components. Instead of relying on introspective reports, they use the Attention Network Test to obtain response-time and accuracy difference scores before and after training. The design compares three groups: meditation-naive participants in an 8-week MBSR course, experienced meditators in a 1-month intensive retreat, and meditation-naive controls who received no mindfulness training. The strongest claim is not that mindfulness improves all attention uniformly, but that different training contexts align with different attentional operations. At baseline, the retreat group already showed less conflict-monitoring interference than the meditation-naive participants, consistent with a link between prior concentrative practice and response-level selection. At Time 2, MBSR participants showed stronger orienting response-time benefits than controls and retreat participants, supporting the idea that repeated practice of engaging and returning attention can improve endogenous orienting. The retreat group, by contrast, showed reduced alerting scores and faster no-cue target detection, which the authors interpret as evidence for a more readied or receptive attentional state rather than reduced alertness. A correlation between prior meditation experience and smaller Time 2 alerting scores strengthens this interpretation. The paper is careful about limits: the retreat group differed in age range and prior experience, the control group was passive rather than active, stress reduction was not separated from attentional training, and repeated task exposure may have affected conflict monitoring. Overall, the source presents preliminary but concrete behavioral evidence that mindfulness practices may tune specific attentional subsystems, with concentrative and receptive modes mapping onto different functions.",
